[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "2607300900"
  :adr/title "GitHub Actions is not executing for this org; move repo-wide CI to the murakumo fleet"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-07-30"
  :adr/tags ["ci" "cd" "fleet-ci" "murakumo" "github-actions" "gates"]
  :adr/supersedes ""
  :adr/superseded-by ""
  :adr/context
  "Asked to move CI/CD repo-wide off GitHub Actions and onto murakumo.cloud. Two things turned out to be true before any design was needed.

First, most of the work already exists. `scripts/fleet-ci/` runs commit-triggered gates on the murakumo mac-mini fleet, signs receipts into an append-only ledger, writes commit statuses back, and advances west pins when green — ADR-2607178000 plus ADR-2607255500. Measured 2026-07-30: 17 gate entries, 23 units per tick including 6 PR heads, fanned across 6 capable nodes. Its README lists PR-head verification as not-done, but tick.cljs implements it (line ~962 closes that item explicitly); the README is stale, not the system.

Second, and decisively: **GitHub Actions is not running at all here.** All 16 workflows in com-junkawasaki/root show `failure` on their latest run, and inspecting those runs shows **zero steps recorded and no logs** — the signature of a job that never started, not of a check that failed. gates.edn already recorded the cause on 2026-07-26: Actions billing stopped for com-junkawasaki and gftdcojp, so jobs do not launch. kotoba-lang/kotoba is now failing too.

So the premise of the request is stronger than a platform preference. There is currently no CI. Sixteen red lights have been standing in for gating, and nothing behind them has run for weeks."
  :adr/decision
  "1. The murakumo fleet (`scripts/fleet-ci/`) is the CI/CD plane for this workspace. GitHub Actions is treated as unavailable, not as a second opinion.

2. Port each workflow's **invariant**, never its implementation. A gate script ships inputs and calls the repo's own verifier; two implementations of one invariant let a check pass on one path and fail on the other unnoticed. This is already the rule for repository-roles and manifest-reality gates.

3. **Credentials never reach a node** (existing invariant 3). This splits every workflow rather than moving it wholesale: the part that needs only the source tree becomes a node-side gate; the part needing GitHub API auth stays operator-side in tick.cljs, which already holds tokens and already runs verify-west-pins before advancing a pin.

4. **Never register a gate that starts red.** A permanently-failing check carries no signal — that is the exact mechanism by which the docs-edn defect was reintroduced twice within hours today (ADR-2607300400). A workflow whose invariant is currently violated gets its breakage fixed first, or gets registered with a declared ratchet, but does not get added as a standing red light.

5. `exit 0` is not a pass. Every ported gate asserts a positive summary from the underlying verifier, as jvm-test asserts `Ran N tests`."
  :adr/consequences
  "Coverage is now 18 gate entries. The invariant that pin regressions are checked at all is restored — it had silently not been running.

Branch protection is the remaining cutover: required status checks still name Actions contexts that never report, so they cannot be satisfied and cannot block anything. Switching them to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*` is what actually makes the fleet authoritative, and it is a repo-settings change, not code.

Two workflows cannot move and need an explicit decision (see body). Until then they stay nominally on Actions, which means they do not run.

Trigger latency stays at the 5-minute LaunchAgent poll rather than a webhook. Acceptable for gates; it means a push waits up to 5 minutes for a verdict."
  :adr/body
  "## The evidence that Actions is dead

| check | result |
|---|---|
| workflows in com-junkawasaki/root | 16, all `runs-on: ubuntu-latest` |
| latest run conclusion | **failure on all 16** |
| steps recorded in those jobs | **0** |
| logs for the failed job | **absent** (`log not found`) |
| contrast | kotoba-lang/kotoba latest 3 runs also `failure` |

Zero steps and no logs mean the runner never picked the job up. Combined with the billing note already in gates.edn, this is a startup failure, not a verification failure.

## Workflow → fleet mapping

**Already on the fleet (3).** docs-edn-verify (as `docs-edn-check`, scoped to `90-docs/adr`), kotoba-repository-roles (same verifier), repo-reality-verify's structural half (as `manifest-reality-check`).

**Ported here (1).** west-pin-verify's policy job → `gates/west-pin-policy-check.cljs`. Its second job (server-side reachability of changed pins) is deliberately not ported: it needs GitHub API auth, which invariant 3 forbids on a node — and it is already operator-side, since tick.cljs runs `verify-west-pins.cljs` before every CD pin advance. What was missing was only the auth-free policy layer, which nothing was running.

**Portable, not yet ported (9).** foundation-deps-verify, public-runtime-owners, fleet-projection-verify, istore-freeze-check, tree-collapse-guard, clj-wgsl-parity, cloud-itonami-live-observe, annex-custody-verify (needs `git-annex` provisioned via brew rather than apt), and the full-scope docs-edn check. Each is a thin gate script plus one gates.edn line.

**Blocked on their own breakage — decision 4 applies.** Measured directly, with real exit codes: `check-foundation-deps.cljs` rc=1, `90-docs/repo-reality/ci_gate.cljs` rc=1, `verify-public-runtime-owners.mjs` rc=1. Only `west-pin-guard-policy-test.cljs` passed (rc=0), which is why it is the one ported. These invariants are genuinely violated right now; nobody has seen them since Actions stopped. Fix, then register.

*(Method note: the first attempt at that measurement read `$?` after a pipe into `tail`, so every script looked like rc=0. The fleet-ci README's own invariant — do not trust exit 0 — applies to the person doing the survey too.)*

**Write/bot workflows, need care (2).** repo-maturity-regenerate and production-gates-bot mutate the repo rather than checking it. fleet-ci has a landing path (branch + contents PUT + server-side merge, never direct push to main) so they fit, but a bot that commits on every tick is a different risk class from a gate and should be opt-in per repo.

**Cannot move (2).**
- `codeql-analysis` — `github/codeql-action` is a hosted GitHub service. A self-hosted CodeQL CLI is possible but is a separate project with its own licensing question. Options: accept no SAST, or adopt a scanner that runs as a plain binary on a node.
- `jupyterlite` — the *build* is portable, but it ends in `actions/upload-pages-artifact` and deploys to GitHub Pages, which is a GitHub product. Moving it means publishing somewhere else (Cloudflare Pages is already used in this workspace).

## Why the fleet architecture is the right target

Worth stating because it is not obvious from outside: the fleet does things Actions did not.

- Receipts are **signed** into an append-only ledger (`manifest/fleet-ci.edn`), with a weak-permission key that can only append — it holds neither pin-advance nor governance quorum.
- Green **advances the west pin** through server-side verification, so CI and CD are one path.
- `exit 0` is structurally distrusted: extraction success and a test summary are both asserted, after a real false-pass incident where a `clojure` that dropped to a REPL returned 0.
- Nodes hold **no credentials**; the operator fetches private trees and streams tarballs over ssh stdin.

## Remaining work, in order

1. Fix the three failing invariants above, then register them as gates (decision 4).
2. Switch branch-protection required checks to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*`. Until this happens nothing is enforced, whichever plane runs.
3. Decide codeql and jupyterlite.
4. Issue the two fine-grained PATs so the LaunchAgent stops skipping (GitHub Web UI, human step) — otherwise ticks only run from an interactive session.
5. Refresh `scripts/fleet-ci/README.md`, which still lists PR-head verification as not-done."
  :adr/verification
  "The ported gate was proved to both pass and fail before registering it:

- against the real tree: rc=0, `west-pin-guard-policy: 3 cases OK`
- tree missing the policy test → rc=**90** (`missing after extract`)
- tree missing `scripts/nbb_compat` → rc=**90** (classpath would be broken)
- verifier present but emitting no summary → rc=**93** (`refusing to report a pass`)

A gate that cannot fail is worth nothing, so the negative paths were exercised rather than assumed. `tick.cljs --plan` then reported coverage 17 → **18 covered**, confirming registration.

Not verified: node-side execution of this gate. That needs a real tick against the mac minis, which the LaunchAgent cannot do until the two PATs exist; the gate was therefore exercised locally against the same tree shape a node receives."
  :adr/resume
  "1. `nbb scripts/fleet-ci/tick.cljs --only root --dry-run` — run the new gate on a real node once credentials allow, confirming the shipped-tree path matches the local run.
2. Fix `check-foundation-deps.cljs`, `90-docs/repo-reality/ci_gate.cljs`, `verify-public-runtime-owners.mjs` (all rc=1 today), then add one gates.edn line each.
3. Switch branch protection to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*`; nothing is enforced until this is done.
4. Owner decisions: codeql replacement, and where jupyterlite publishes.
5. Issue the two fine-grained PATs (README lists the exact scopes)."
  :adr/evidence
  "gates.edn now carries 18 entries; gates/west-pin-policy-check.cljs added. Measured against com-junkawasaki/root main on 2026-07-30. Prior art: ADR-2607178000 (standing runner), ADR-2607255500 (tip-driven CI/CD), ADR-2607300400 (why a permanently-red gate carries no signal)."}]
